A||||12||2birds||88 suspension swap?||||1
Z||000000||2birds||11-21-1999||01:44 AM||||Does anybody have any experience or info on swapping 88 suspension into an earlier model?   Is it even possible?  Any input is appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula||11-21-1999||08:56 AM||||I seriously doubt that it would make a huge difference. but, thats my opinion||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero||11-21-1999||11:06 AM||||You can swap the suspension on a '88 to an earlier model but it's too much work involve...as fas as difference in ride...the'88 has a better ride than the older models...||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||11-21-1999||12:43 PM||||Are the rear cradle mounting points the same? Does the front suspension come off as a unit, like the rear?  I was looking under a 88 Formula I found in a junkyard and noticed the heater hoses are routed differently, and the main coolant pipes are different around the front crossmember.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||11-21-1999||04:36 PM||||The complete cradle assembly (includes entire rear suspension) from an 88 can be installed into older Fieros. The only real problem is properly locating the strut towers.  Ryan Motorsports makes brakets specifically for this purpose:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/88cradle.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/88cradle.htm</A> <P>As for the 88 front suspension swap, it just isnt even worth considering due to the extreme amount of re-engineering involved.<P>Mike<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||11-21-1999||05:09 PM||||I might be wrong, but I thought somebody (maybe Greendano?) stated in an earlier thread that the entire front suspension assembly from a 88 was a direct bolt-in swap into the earlier cars.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||11-21-1999||05:47 PM||||Quite to the contrary, Batboy, this swap requires enough welding and cutting to give even the best of us a migraine.  It just isnt worth it!  This discussion has been done over and over on the other lists, and in every case, the conclusions were as I implied.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tim||11-21-1999||05:56 PM||||The 88 front is part of the frame. The only way to get an 88 front is to use an 88 complete chassis!||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||11-21-1999||06:21 PM||||I have fieros with both suspensions (88 formula and 87 coupe) and the only differance is that the 88 rides a little better. You would be better off going with wider tires if you are looking for more cornering grip and a rear sway bar to help quench oversteer. I forgot to say that the brakes are a hole lot better but you could go to the after market for that if you want more stopping power. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||11-21-1999||07:38 PM||||Ryane also has a complete replacement front suspension for the pre-88 cars. Bring lots of money. <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-21-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||11-21-1999||09:39 PM||||Thanks for the info on the swap.  I currently have 225/60/15 on the rear, and have lowered it and added a sway bar and polyurethane, but the trailing throttle oversteer is still there.  This is fixed by sane driving, but the bumpsteer is the real problem I'd like to solve.  Has anyone fitted larger tires than this with stock GT wheels?  I have 215/60/15 on the front, and they will rub at full lock.||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||11-21-1999||10:24 PM||||Make sure you have a good alignment for that rear steer problem. Check your control arms for looseness. Urethane bushing for the cradle and control arms help out too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||pherder||11-22-1999||08:16 PM||||2birds,<P>RCC has a kit to reduce the 84-87 rear bump steer for $120.<P>Others have kits also (i.e. Joe Wymann details how to reduce the bump steer in his CD book but it requires a lot of welding and destroying of parts...) but RCC's just bolts on.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/rcc" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/rcc</A> <P>||||reg||3
